            </owl:Class>
        </owl:equivalentClass>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0032501"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0098727"/>
        <rdfs:subClassOf>
            <owl:Restriction>
                <owl:onProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/RO_0012003"/>
                <owl:someValuesFrom rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/CL_0000034"/>
            </owl:Restriction>
        </rdfs:subClassOf>
        <obo:IAO_0000115>The process by which an organism or tissue maintains a population of stem cells of a single type. This can be achieved by a number of mechanisms: stem cell asymmetric division maintains stem cell numbers; stem cell symmetric division increases them; maintenance of a stem cell niche maintains the conditions for commitment to the stem cell fate for some types of stem cell; stem cells may arise de novo from other cell types.</obo:IAO_0000115>
        <oboI:hasOBONamespace>biological_process</oboI:hasOBONamespace>
        <oboI:id>GO:0019827</oboI:id>
        <rdfs:label>stem cell population maintenance</rdfs:label>
    </owl:Class>
    <owl:Axiom>
        <owl:annotatedSource rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/GO_0019827"/>
        <owl:annotatedProperty rdf:resource="http://purl.obolibrary.org/obo/IAO_0000115"/>
        <owl:annotatedTarget>The process by which an organism or tissue maintains a population of stem cells of a single type. This can be achieved by a number of mechanisms: stem cell asymmetric division maintains stem cell numbers; stem cell symmetric division increases them; maintenance of a stem cell niche maintains the conditions for commitment to the stem cell fate for some types of stem cell; stem cells may arise de novo from other cell types.</owl:annotatedTarget>
        <oboI:hasDbXref>GOC:mah</oboI:hasDbXref>
        <oboI:hasDbXref>ISBN:0878932437</oboI:hasDbXref>
    </owl:Axiom>
    


